      "url": "https://www.last.fm/music/Marillion/marillion.com",
      "wiki_content": "marillion.com is Marillion's 11th studio album. Released in late 1999, it was self-produced with additional production from Porcupine Tree's Steven Wilson on five out of nine tracks. The album contains a variety of different styles, ranging from epic prog rock (\"Interior Lulu\") through (by Marillion's standards) lighthearted pop (\"Deserve\", \"Rich\") to trip-hop influences (\"House\"). Due to its lack of stylistic cohesion, the album received some relatively critical feedback from the fanbase.\n\nThe album's title is a reference to Marillion's pioneering approach in using the internet to communicate with their fans. While preparing the album, the band's management had invited fans to send them passport photographs to be featured inside the booklet.\n\nThe tracks \"Tumble Down The Years\" and \"Interior Lulu\" were originally written for the Radiation album.\n\nThere were no singles released officially from this album, but \"Deserve\" was released as a promo single, and \"Rich\" served as a radio single in Brazil. There was also a video made for the track \"Deserve\", featuring tour and backstage footage.\n\nMarillion made a companion disc to marillion.com, which was named marillion.co.uk and was available for free to everyone who bought the album. The companion disc had a collection of tracks from various live and demo releases, as well as a short interview video (which was originally from a press kit for the album). The companion disc has been regularly updated with newer songs and is still available from Marillion's official website.\n <a href=\"https://www.last.fm/music/Marillion/marillion.com\">Read more on Last.fm</a>. User-contributed text is available under the Creative Commons By-SA License; additional terms may apply.",
      "wiki_summary": "marillion.com is Marillion's 11th studio album. Released in late 1999, it was self-produced with additional production from Porcupine Tree's Steven Wilson on five out of nine tracks. The album contains a variety of different styles, ranging from epic prog rock (\"Interior Lulu\") through (by Marillion's standards) lighthearted pop (\"Deserve\", \"Rich\") to trip-hop influences (\"House\"). Due to its lack of stylistic cohesion, the album received some relatively critical feedback from the fanbase. The album's title is a reference to Marillion's pioneering approach in using the internet to communicate with their fans. While preparing the album, the band's management had invited fans <a href=\"https://www.last.fm/music/Marillion/marillion.com\">Read more on Last.fm</a>."
    },
    "perplexity": {
      "description": "Marillion.com is the 1999 self-titled seventh studio album by the British rock band Marillion, originally released as an independent statement of artistic freedom that captured the band at a moment of bold reinvention. Recorded at their own studio, The Racket Club, between December 1998 and August 1999, the album’s zeitgeisty title reflected Marillion’s embrace of the internet as a direct communication channel with fans, leading to early forays into crowdfunding. The music moves effortlessly between cinematic arrangements, emotional storytelling, and moments of intimate reflection, embracing expansive songwriting, atmospheric depth, and an openness to global musical influences that continues to resonate with fans old and new.\n\nIn June 2026, earMUSIC released a deluxe reissue of Marillion.com as a 3CD+Blu-ray media book and a 5LP box set, celebrating one of the band’s most distinctive and forward-thinking releases. This expanded edition features a newly mixed and mastered version of the original studio album by Michael Hunter, a previously unreleased complete recording of the band’s 1999 performance at Shepherd’s Bush Empire in London, and a bonus Blu-ray containing the album in hi-res and surround sound, demos, early versions, a documentary, and the “Shot In The Dark (Zodiac)” concert video. The sets also include illustrated booklets with rare photos, new artwork, memorabilia, and an essay digging deep into the album’s story, offering a richly textured listening experience that honors the original’s legacy while providing significant new content for collectors.",
